# STEP 1 — Baseline: with no voice policy and no MED endpoint, the DUT sends NO MED TLVs

**Why this step.** LLDP-MED is an extension that only appears under conditions. Before
creating those conditions, establish that the thing being tested is genuinely absent — so that
its later appearance is a measurement and not a pre-existing state.

**Measurement**, a 40 s capture on eth3 with the bench untouched:
```
pcap      /tmp/lldp1159/m1_base.pcap
source e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 01:80:c2:00:00:0e
  --- frame at 1792550955.713, 4 TLVs ---
    Chassis ID                         MAC address: e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43
    Port ID                            Interface name: port1.0.10
    Time To Live                       120 s
    OUI e01aea subtype 2               0c26573a87c541829d436cb04a0e77c2
```
**Four TLVs: the three mandatory ones plus the Allied Telesis OUI TLV. No MED TLV of any
kind**, even though `show lldp interface` reports all four MED TLVs selected (`McNpLoPe--`).

**STEP 1 verdict: PASS** (baseline established — MED absent).

---

# STEP 2 — Configuring a voice VLAN creates a Network Policy, but does NOT put it on the wire

**Why this step.** This is the discriminator that tells "configured" from "advertised", and it
is exactly the `platforms/x960.md` §5 trap: the `show` command will say the policy exists while
the wire may carry nothing.

**Configuration sent:**
```
awplus(config)#vlan database
awplus(config-vlan)#vlan 70 name ckVOICE
awplus(config-vlan)#vlan 70 state enable
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.10
awplus(config-if)#switchport voice vlan 70
awplus(config-if)#switchport voice vlan priority 5
awplus(config-if)#switchport voice dscp 46
```
No `% ` line on any of them.

**What the configuration did, read back:**
```
awplus#show vlan brief
70      ckVOICE          STATIC  ACTIVE  port1.0.10(t)
awplus#show running-config interface port1.0.10
interface port1.0.10
 switchport
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 10
 spanning-tree portfast
 switchport voice vlan 70
 switchport voice dscp 46
awplus#show lldp local-info med interface port1.0.10
  Network Policy ................... Voice
       VLAN ID ..................... 70
       Tagged Flag ................. Tagged
       Layer-2 Priority ............ 5
       DSCP Value .................. 46
```

🔬 **Three things worth recording here, and two of them are traps.**
1. **The voice VLAN joins the port TAGGED (`port1.0.10(t)`) while the access VLAN stays
   untagged.** The inherited underlay was not disturbed, and that was verified rather than
   assumed: `tools/tb ping tb277 eth3 10.38.22.34 3` → **3 received, 0% packet loss** with the
   voice VLAN in place. A voice-VLAN case that breaks the management path it is measured over
   would produce nonsense.
2. 🔥 **`switchport voice vlan` silently adds `spanning-tree portfast` to the interface.** It
   was not in the pre-case running-config and nobody typed it. It is a **teardown hazard**: a
   tester who removes only what they typed leaves it behind, and the running-config diff at
   the end of the case then fails for a reason that looks unrelated. This case removes it
   explicitly (see Teardown).
3. 🔬 **`switchport voice vlan priority 5` was accepted and NOT written back** — it is absent
   from running-config above, while `switchport voice dscp 46` is present. 5 is the default
   voice priority, and only a non-default value is stored. STEP 5 confirms this from the other
   side by setting priority 6 and watching it appear.

**Measurement — and the point of the step.** A 40 s capture with the policy fully configured
and **no MED endpoint on the port**:
```
pcap      /tmp/lldp1159/m3_novoiceend.pcap
  --- frame at 1792551045.796, 4 TLVs ---
    Chassis ID / Port ID / Time To Live / OUI e01aea subtype 2
```
**Still four TLVs. The Network Policy the DUT says it has is NOT on the wire.** `show lldp
local-info med` reports a fully formed voice policy; the frames carry nothing of it. That is
correct TIA-1057 behaviour — a switch advertises MED TLVs to a port only once it has seen an
LLDP-MED endpoint there — and it is precisely the kind of gap that a `show`-based verdict would
have missed in the opposite direction.

**STEP 2 verdict: PASS.**

---

# STEP 3 — A real LLDP-MED endpoint makes the DUT advertise the policy, and the policy is correct

**What was done.**
```
tools/tb sudotool tb277 lldp1159 lldpmed_phone eth3 /tmp/lldp1159/m4_phone.pcap \
    --lldp-secs 14 --no-dhcp
15:51:16 phase 1: LLDP-MED from 00:00:5e:00:53:10 on eth3 for 14s
  sent 14 LLDPDUs
  voice network policy: {'app': 1, 'unknown': 0, 'tagged': 1, 'vlan': 70, 'l2prio': 5, 'dscp': 46}
no DHCP phase (vlan=70)
exit 0
```

**The DUT's reply, decoded independently with `lldpwire.py` from the same pcap:**
```
pcap      /tmp/lldp1159/m4_phone.pcap
packets   57 total, 3 LLDP from e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43

source e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 01:80:c2:00:00:0e
  frames 3 over 2.0 s
  inter-frame gap  min 1.00 s  mean 1.00 s  max 1.00 s
  --- frame at 1792551077.827, 7 TLVs ---
    Chassis ID                         MAC address: e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43
    Port ID                            Interface name: port1.0.10
    Time To Live                       120 s
    OUI e01aea subtype 2               0c26573a87c541829d436cb04a0e77c2
    802.3 MAC/PHY Configuration/Status 038001001e
    MED LLDP-MED Capabilities          caps=[Capabilities, Network Policy, Location ID, Inventory] device type=Network Connectivity
    MED Network Policy                 application=Voice vlan=70 tagged=True priority=5 dscp=46 unknown_policy=False
```
(all three frames identical; the other two at 1792551078.829 and 1792551079.830)

**What this proves, field by field.** The advertisement went from **4 TLVs to 7**, and every
new field matches what was configured:

| configured | advertised in the frame | match |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `switchport voice vlan 70` | Network Policy `vlan=70`, `tagged=True` | ✅ |
| `switchport voice vlan priority 5` | Network Policy `priority=5` | ✅ |
| `switchport voice dscp 46` | Network Policy `dscp=46` | ✅ |
| (application type) | `application=Voice` (TIA app type 1) | ✅ |
| the DUT is a switch | MED Capabilities `device type=Network Connectivity` | ✅ |
| `show lldp local-info med` capability list | `caps=[Capabilities, Network Policy, Location ID, Inventory]` | ✅ |

🔬 The **802.3 MAC/PHY Configuration/Status** TLV also appears, although no `lldp tlv-select
mac-phy-config` was configured. LLDP-MED requires it of a Network Connectivity device, so the
DUT adds it on its own when it enters MED mode on a port. Worth knowing: an 802.3 TLV can
appear on a port whose 802.3 selection column reads `--------`.

**Two independent decoders agree.** `lldpmed_phone.py` parsed the Network Policy as
`{'app': 1, 'tagged': 1, 'vlan': 70, 'l2prio': 5, 'dscp': 46}` and `lldpwire.py`, written
separately, parsed the same bytes as `application=Voice vlan=70 tagged=True priority=5
dscp=46`. The 12-bit VLAN / 3-bit priority / 6-bit DSCP packing is the easiest thing in LLDP-MED
to decode wrongly, and two tools built independently from the standard read it identically.

**STEP 3 verdict: PASS.**

