# STEP 5 — LLDP-MED Fast Start: exactly three frames, one second apart, and what triggers it

**Why this step.** `show lldp` reports `Fast Start Count ... 3`. Fast start exists so a phone
plugged into a port learns its voice VLAN in seconds instead of waiting out a 30 s timer. It is
a **timing** behaviour, so it can only be graded from frame timestamps.

**The measurement, from the same captures as STEP 3:**
```
source e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 01:80:c2:00:00:0e
  frames 3 over 2.0 s
  inter-frame gap  min 1.00 s  mean 1.00 s  max 1.00 s
```
**Exactly three frames, at 1.00 s with no spread**, against this DUT's steady-state rate of
**30.03 s** (measured repeatedly in T44296 on the same port). `Fast Start Count 3` is three
frames, and the fast-start interval is one second.

**What triggers it — four observations, one rule.** The burst did not happen every time a MED
frame arrived, so the trigger was pinned down rather than assumed:

| run | MED endpoint | was it NEW to the port? | burst? |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `m4_phone` `...53:10` | first appearance | **yes** | **3 frames @ 1.00 s** |
| `m8_prio6` `...53:10` | re-appeared after its TTL-120 entry had expired at ≈1792551197 | **yes** | **3 frames @ 1.00 s** |
| `m9_nopolicy` `...53:10` | entry still live from `m8` (valid to ≈1792551376) | **no** | **none in a 10 s window** |
| `m11_newmac` `...53:20` | a different MAC, so a different endpoint | **yes** | **3 frames @ 1.00 s** |

🔬 **The rule the evidence supports: the fast-start burst fires when an LLDP-MED endpoint
becomes NEW on the port, not on every MED frame received.** `m11` is the decisive one — it
used a different endpoint MAC to create a new neighbour on demand, rather than waiting out a
TTL, and the burst fired immediately. (It also fired with the Network Policy TLV deselected, so
fast start is triggered by the endpoint, not by having a policy to send.)

**And a correction to an inference I nearly made.** A 40 s capture taken at 1792551212
(`m7_prio6.pcap`), with the phone's entry apparently still in the table, showed a **4-TLV**
frame with no MED TLVs — which looked at first like "the DUT only ever sends MED TLVs in the
burst". The arithmetic says otherwise: the phone's last frame was at ≈1792551077 with TTL 120,
so the entry expired at ≈1792551197 — **fifteen seconds before that capture's frame**. There
was no MED endpoint on the port at that moment, so there was correctly no MED TLV. The
70 s capture in STEP 6, taken while an endpoint *was* live, settles it the other way: the
periodic advertisement carries MED TLVs too. **Recorded because the wrong reading was one
unchecked timestamp away.**

**STEP 5 verdict: PASS.**

---

# STEP 6 — The policy follows the configuration: priority 5 → 6 on the wire

**Why this step.** STEP 3 proved one policy was advertised correctly. This proves the
advertisement *tracks* the configuration, and it doubles as the check on the default-priority
observation from STEP 2.

**Configuration sent:** `switchport voice vlan priority 6` on port1.0.10.

**It is now stored**, where `priority 5` was not:
```
awplus#show running-config interface port1.0.10
 switchport voice vlan 70
 switchport voice vlan priority 6        <- present; `priority 5` never was
 switchport voice dscp 46
awplus#show lldp local-info med interface port1.0.10
       Layer-2 Priority ............ 6
```
🔬 Confirms STEP 2's reading: **the voice priority is written back only when it is not the
default of 5.**

**On the wire** (a new endpoint, so a fast-start burst; `m8_prio6.pcap`):
```
  --- frame at 1792551247.982, 7 TLVs ---
    MED Network Policy                 application=Voice vlan=70 tagged=True priority=6 dscp=46 unknown_policy=False
```
`priority=6`. The VLAN and DSCP are unchanged, so exactly the field that was reconfigured is
exactly the field that moved.

**STEP 6 verdict: PASS.**

---

# STEP 7 — Two INDEPENDENT removal paths, each proven on the wire

**Why this step.** `satisfactory-level-of-testing`: the step text is the floor and removal must
always be proven. LLDP-MED has two quite different ways to stop advertising a network policy,
and they are worth telling apart: switching the **TLV off** and removing the **policy's
content**. Both must leave the rest of LLDP-MED working.

## 7a — `no lldp med-tlv-select network-policy` (the TLV selection)

```
awplus(config-if)#no lldp med-tlv-select network-policy
awplus#show lldp interface port1.0.10
 1.0.10   Rx Tx  -- --  10.38.22.34      ---------- -------- -------- Mc--LoPe--
```
`Np` is gone from the MED column; `Mc`, `Lo`, `Pe` remain. The voice VLAN configuration is
untouched (`switchport voice vlan 70 / priority 6 / dscp 46` all still in running-config).

**On the wire, to a NEW endpoint (`m11_newmac.pcap`, MAC `...53:20`):**
```
  frames 3 over 2.0 s        <- fast start still fires
  --- frame, 6 TLVs ---
    Chassis ID / Port ID / Time To Live / OUI e01aea subtype 2
    802.3 MAC/PHY Configuration/Status 038001001e
    MED LLDP-MED Capabilities          caps=[Capabilities, Network Policy, Location ID, Inventory] device type=Network Connectivity
```
**Seven TLVs became six: the Network Policy TLV is gone and MED Capabilities remains.** The
endpoint-side tool agrees independently: `voice network policy: None`.

**And on the PERIODIC advertisement too** (`m10_nopolicy_periodic.pcap`, a 70 s capture with a
MED endpoint live on the port):
```
  frames 2 over 30.0 s
  inter-frame gap  min 30.03 s  mean 30.03 s  max 30.03 s
  --- frame at 1792551340.062, 6 TLVs ---
    ... MED LLDP-MED Capabilities ... (no Network Policy TLV)
```
Two things at once: the removal holds in the **steady-state** advertisement as well as in the
burst, and — the point STEP 5 flagged — **while a MED endpoint is live, the ordinary 30 s
frames do carry MED TLVs**. Ordinary LLDP is completely unaffected: still 30.03 s, still TTL
120.

**The arc closes.** `lldp med-tlv-select network-policy` restored → `McNpLoPe--` again, and to
a new endpoint (`...53:30`):
```
MED-network-policy {'app': 1, 'unknown': 0, 'tagged': 1, 'vlan': 70, 'l2prio': 6, 'dscp': 46}
```
The policy is back, with the priority-6 value from STEP 6 intact.

## 7b — `no switchport voice vlan` (the policy's content)

```
awplus(config-if)#no switchport voice vlan
awplus#show lldp local-info med interface port1.0.10
  LLDP-MED Capabilities ............ LLDP-MED Capabilities, Network Policy, ...
  Network Policy ................... [not configured]
```
Back to the STEP 2 baseline wording. **On the wire, to a NEW endpoint
(`m14_novlan.pcap`, MAC `...53:40`):**
```
  frames 3 over 2.0 s        <- fast start still fires
  --- frame at 1792551505.211, 6 TLVs ---
    ... MED LLDP-MED Capabilities ... (no Network Policy TLV)
```
**Six TLVs again — the same wire result as 7a, reached by a completely different route.** The
TLV is still *selected* (`Np` is in the column) but there is no policy to put in it, so none is
sent. A device that advertised an empty or stale policy here would be a defect; this one
advertises nothing.

🔬 **A teardown hazard, measured:** after `no switchport voice vlan`, the lines
`switchport voice vlan priority 6` and `switchport voice dscp 46` **remain in running-config**
with no voice VLAN for them to describe. They are not removed with the VLAN and must be
removed individually. Together with the implicit `spanning-tree portfast` of STEP 2, that is
**three lines a careless teardown leaves behind**, none of which the tester typed as a group.

**STEP 7 verdict: PASS** (both removal paths proven, both arcs closed).

