## 🔬 Findings for `platforms/x960.md`

1. 🔥 **`switchport voice vlan` silently adds `spanning-tree portfast` to the interface.** It
   is not typed, it is not announced, and it **is saved by a `write`**. A teardown that removes
   only the voice lines leaves it behind and the running-config diff then fails for a reason
   that looks unrelated to the case.
2. 🔬 **`no switchport voice vlan` does NOT remove `switchport voice vlan priority` or
   `switchport voice dscp`.** Both survive with no voice VLAN to describe, and must be removed
   individually.
3. 🔬 **`switchport voice vlan priority 5` is accepted but not written back** — 5 is the
   default and only a non-default priority is stored. Proven both ways: priority 5 absent from
   running-config while advertised as 5 on the wire; priority 6 present in running-config and
   advertised as 6.
4. **The MED TLVs are selected by default** (`McNpLoPe--` on an untouched port) but a MED TLV
   reaches the wire **only while an LLDP-MED endpoint is in the neighbour table for that
   port**. With a voice policy fully configured and no endpoint present, the advertisement is
   the plain 4-TLV one.
5. **LLDP-MED fast start fires when an endpoint becomes NEW on the port**, not on every MED
   frame received: exactly **3 frames at 1.00 s** (matching `Fast Start Count 3`) against a
   steady state of 30.03 s. Measured four times, including once with a deliberately different
   endpoint MAC to create a new neighbour on demand.
6. **Entering MED mode adds the 802.3 MAC/PHY Configuration/Status TLV on its own**, although
   `show lldp interface`'s 802.3 column reads `--------`. LLDP-MED requires it of a Network
   Connectivity device.
7. **The AT-x960-56YSQ reports `Extended Power Via MDI (PoE) ..... [not supported]`** in its
   own MED local-info — it has no PoE — while correctly reading an endpoint's
   `Extended Power - PD` capability. The `Pe` TLV is selected by default and has nothing to say.
8. **The voice VLAN joins an access port TAGGED** (`port1.0.10(t)`) and leaves the access VLAN
   untagged; IP connectivity over the access VLAN is unaffected (verified 3/3 by ping).
9. **`show interface <port> switchport` does not exist on this build** (`% Invalid input`).
   Use `show vlan brief`.
10. **An x960-56YSQ cold-boots to its login prompt in 60 s**, measured a second time here,
    independently of T44296's measurement.

## 🔧 Tooling notes

- **`tools/lldpmed_phone.py` is now hardware-verified.** Its status had read *"generalised
  2026-10-02, not re-verified on hardware"* since it was moved into `tools/`. Six runs here
  against a real switch: it sent its LLDPDUs, decoded the switch's Network Policy correctly
  every time (agreeing byte-for-byte with the independently written `lldpwire.py`), returned
  `voice network policy: None` when there was none, and `--mac` gave on-demand new endpoints,
  which is what pinned down the fast-start trigger. `--no-dhcp` is the right mode on a bench
  with no DHCP server in the voice VLAN. Its README status is updated.
- **`tools/rcdiff.py` emits a spurious `-HH:MM:SS` line** when the `show running-config` in a
  `ckcon` stdout is immediately followed by another command's timestamp header. Cosmetic, but
  it means an "empty" diff can print one line; check what the line is before treating it as
  config drift. Noted in its README entry.

## What was NOT measured, and why

- **`swi_a.cfg` and `swi_b.cfg`** — both partner consoles are locked out (above). Neither
  switch is part of this case's topology beyond being a neighbour on another port.
- **LLDP-MED Location Identification (`Lo`)** — the TLV is selected by default and reads
  `[not configured]`. Configuring a civic or ELIN location was not attempted; it is a distinct
  feature with its own CLI and deserves its own case.
- **Extended Power-via-MDI (`Pe`)** — **not testable on this unit**: the AT-x960-56YSQ has no
  PoE and says so itself (`[not supported]`). Not a failure, and not a gap that any bench
  change here could close.
- **`lldp med-notifications`** — the MED Topology Change trap (`TC` in the `show lldp
  interface` Notif column). T44296 proved the LLDP trap path end to end
  (`lldpRemTablesChange` caught on the wire with a zero-trap control), so the SNMP transport is
  known good; the MED-specific notification was not separately exercised.
- **`lldp faststart-count` was not varied.** The default of 3 was confirmed on the wire four
  times; changing it to another value and re-measuring would add breadth, not confidence.
- **The DHCP phase of `lldpmed_phone.py`** — skipped with `--no-dhcp` deliberately: there is no
  DHCP server in VLAN 70 on this bench, so the phase could only ever have returned "no OFFER",
  which measures the bench and not the DUT.
- **`switchport voice vlan dot1p`, `untagged` and `dynamic`** — read off the CLI surface and
  recorded, not exercised. `dynamic` needs RADIUS-assigned voice VLAN, which is an AAA case.

## VERDICT

**Run: clean** (not confounded — see HEALTH sample 2).

**VERDICT: PASS -- LLDP-MED works on the AT-x960-56YSQ and every limb was graded on frames, not
on `show lldp local-info med`: the DUT types itself Network Connectivity and advertises NO MED
TLV until an LLDP-MED endpoint appears on the port, which is the §5 read-back trap caught in
the honest direction (a fully configured voice policy, 40 s of capture, zero MED TLVs on the
wire); presented with a real Class III endpoint it advertised a Network Policy TLV carrying
exactly the configured application=Voice vlan=70 tagged priority=5 dscp=46, decoded identically
by two independently written tools, and classified the endpoint as Endpoint Class III /
Telephone against a plain Station-only neighbour on the SAME port at the SAME instant that got
blank MED columns; LLDP-MED Fast Start measured at exactly 3 frames 1.00 s apart against a
30.03 s steady state, with the trigger pinned to a NEW endpoint by four observations including
one driven with a deliberately different endpoint MAC; the advertisement tracked a priority
5 -> 6 reconfiguration on the wire; BOTH removal paths proven independently -- deselecting the
TLV and removing the voice VLAN each took the advertisement from 7 TLVs to 6 while leaving MED
Capabilities, ordinary LLDP and fast start intact -- and both arcs closed; and 🔴 REBOOT
PERSISTENCE MEASURED, with vlan 70, tagged, a NON-DEFAULT priority 6 and dscp 46 all surviving
a cold boot and proven in a frame. Three configuration traps found, one of them a real teardown
hazard (`switchport voice vlan` silently adds `spanning-tree portfast`). The bench was returned
byte-for-byte identical to the state group C inherited, the closing probe exits 0 MATCH, and
the only deliverables that could not be produced are the partner switches' .cfg files, because
both partner consoles are locked out.**
